            0.06
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cell: The Building Block of Life"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "21 N"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.775,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe? Do you observe the cells of the onion root tip? Are they similar in structure? Do you find any structural differences in these cells? If yes, why is it so?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.142,
            0.775,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This is because the cells of a growing tip of root of onion divide continuously. This process is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cell division",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Fig. 2.17 shows various stages of cell division. Therefore, these cells exhibit different structures corresponding to different stages of cell division. Can you identify which stage comes first during cell division?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.23,
            0.775,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Every day, an estimated hundreds of billions of cells in our body are replaced, which is almost 1 per cent of the total number of cells in our body. Both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells divide, but eukaryotic cells divide in a more controlled and orderly manner by a process called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cell cycle",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". You will learn about the stages of cell division in higher grades."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.321,
            0.789,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.095,
                0.321,
                0.789,
                0.49
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.278,
                0.496,
                0.722,
                0.511
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 2.17: Different stages of cell division in onion root tip cells"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.521,
            0.316,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.4.1 Cell division"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.543,
            0.776,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cell division is the process by which new cells are formed from pre‑existing cells. It allows living organisms to grow, repair damaged tissues and reproduce. Some cells, such as skin cells divide continuously to replace cells that are lost regularly. There are two major types of cell division — "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "mitosis and meiosis",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Mitosis is important for normal growth, repair, maintenance and asexual reproduction, while meiosis is important for sexual reproduction for creation of genetic diversity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.671,
            0.198,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mitosis"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.693,
            0.524,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Every human begins life as a single fertilised egg. This one cell divides repeatedly to form trillions of cells in the body. Cells increase in number through mitosis, which is the most common type of cell division (Fig. 2.18)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.783,
            0.525,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mitosis produces two genetically identical daughter cells from one parent cell. Each new cell gets the same DNA and the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell. This ensures that genetic information is largely maintained across body cells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.548,
            0.678,
            0.773,
            0.875
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
